{
  "result": "The train station kept its lost property in a room behind the ticket office. On Tuesdays, when business was slow, Mara took everything out and dusted the shelves. She liked the umbrellas best. Opened indoors, they made the room look briefly like a place where weather happened.\n\nOne morning a man came in looking for a blue scarf. Mara brought him three. He examined each in turn, not touching them, then said he couldn\u2019t remember which blue it had been. His wife had always called it the good scarf. He had never needed another name for it.\n\nShe set the scarves on the counter and went back to sorting keys. After a while the man picked up the plainest one. There was a repair near the fringe, a little ladder of stitches in purple thread. He slid his thumb over it twice.\n\n\u201cThat\u2019s not mine,\u201d he said. \u201cShe would have used blue.\u201d Then he folded it carefully, lining up the ends, and asked if he could wait there until his train came. Mara moved a box of single gloves off the spare chair. They sat without talking while the loudspeaker announced delays.\n\nAfter he left, Mara put the scarves away. On the inventory card she had written BLUE three times. She crossed out the last entry and wrote BLUE, PURPLE MENDING. The next Tuesday she did the same for a coat with one wooden button. By the end of the month, she needed bigger cards for the umbrellas as well.",
